## Key Concepts

**XOR Inversion Test Point (ITP)**
A single XOR gate inserted on a hard-to-control net. When the scan-loaded control input is 0, the gate is transparent (normal operation). When set to 1 during BIST, it inverts the signal, compensating for random pattern bias toward one logic value.

**SCOAP Metrics**
Sandia Controllability/Observability Analysis Program metrics:
- **CC0** — combinational cost to set a node to logic 0
- **CC1** — combinational cost to set a node to logic 1
- **CO** — combinational cost to observe a node at a primary output

**NC fault (Not Controlled)**
A fault where random patterns cannot adequately drive the node to both logic values, leaving stuck-at faults undetected. Primary target for XOR ITP insertion.

**BIST (Built-In Self-Test)**
An on-chip test architecture where test pattern generation and response evaluation are performed by dedicated hardware, typically using an LFSR for pseudo-random pattern generation.

---

## Circuits

| Circuit | Description | Gate count (approx) |
|---|---|---|
| b14 | Complex ALU with registers | ~10K |
| b15 | Memory controller | ~10K |
| b17 | More complex sequential circuit | ~32K |

All circuits are from the **ITC'99 benchmark suite**, synthesised and scan-inserted prior to this study.

